            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.3.2 How are various parts connected in our body?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.654,
            0.504,
            0.909
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have read that blood connects different parts of the body by transporting nutrients, gases, hormones, etc. Grade 7 In the same way, bones connect Curiosity Chapter 9 and support the body from head to toe. A tissue that connects and supports other tissues is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "connective tissue",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Both blood and bones are connective tissues. Though both are connective tissues, they differ in composition and consistency. Blood is fluid, while bone is hard. This difference is due to the matrix, which is watery, soft and jelly‑like in blood but hard, solid, and rigid in bones (Fig. 3.12a)."
            }
          ]
        },
